1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:06,052
Welcome to Episode Eight, subtopic seven point five: the Judas Swing.

2
00:00:06,472 --> 00:00:12,880
This concept brings together everything so far: liquidity, timing, and Daily Bias.

3
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:19,972
Our main goal is precision: knowing when the label applies, and when an ordinary sweep is just a sweep.

4
00:00:21,792 --> 00:00:33,960
A Judas Swing is an ICT interpretation of an initial, counter-directional move early in a session, that runs liquidity before price moves in the direction of the Daily Bias.

5
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:39,516
The name suggests betrayal: the early move misleads traders who follow it.

6
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:47,640
It is session-based. The London and New York opens are where it is studied most, often measured from the New York midnight open.

7
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:50,580
It usually involves a liquidity sweep.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,408
It requires a Daily Bias set before the move. Otherwise, “counter-directional” has no meaning.

9
00:00:57,828 --> 00:01:03,492
And it is confirmed only later, by displacement and structure in the bias direction.

10
00:01:03,912 --> 00:01:10,848
Like manipulation, the term describes price behaviour. It is not proof of deliberate manipulation by anyone.

11
00:01:12,668 --> 00:01:19,700
The bullish Judas Swing model. Step one: the Daily Bias is bullish, established before the session.

12
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,880
Step two: identify the references. The New York midnight open, and the Asian range.

13
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:32,564
Step three: at the London open, price initially moves down, below the midnight open.

14
00:01:32,984 --> 00:01:40,568
Step four: it sweeps sell-side liquidity below the Asian low. This is only a potential Judas Swing at this point.

15
00:01:40,988 --> 00:01:46,772
Step five: the downward move fails to sustain. Price stops making progress lower.

16
00:01:47,192 --> 00:01:52,232
Step six: bullish displacement develops, back above the midnight open.

17
00:01:52,652 --> 00:01:58,004
Step seven: a possible bullish market structure shift above the last lower high.

18
00:01:58,424 --> 00:02:08,696
Step eight: price moves toward buy-side liquidity, here the previous day high. Only now, with context and confirmation, does the Judas Swing label fit.

19
00:02:10,516 --> 00:02:17,932
The bearish Judas Swing is the mirror image. Step one: a bearish Daily Bias, set before the session.

20
00:02:18,352 --> 00:02:22,840
Step two: the midnight open and the Asian range as references.

21
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:27,868
Step three: price initially moves up, above the midnight open.

22
00:02:28,288 --> 00:02:32,728
Step four: it sweeps buy-side liquidity above the Asian high.

23
00:02:33,148 --> 00:02:39,436
Steps five and six: the rally fails to sustain, and bearish displacement follows.

24
00:02:39,856 --> 00:02:43,648
Step seven: a possible bearish market structure shift.

25
00:02:44,068 --> 00:02:47,980
Step eight: price moves toward sell-side liquidity below.

26
00:02:49,800 --> 00:03:01,824
Judas Swings are usually studied around specific times. The New York midnight open is midnight New York time: noon in Malaysia during daylight time, or one p m during standard time.

27
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:12,252
The London window, two to five a m New York, is two to five p m in Malaysia during EDT, and three to six p m during EST.

28
00:03:12,672 --> 00:03:20,784
The New York morning window is seven to ten p m Malaysia time during EDT, and eight to eleven p m during EST.

29
00:03:21,204 --> 00:03:30,996
But timing is context. These are where false moves are most often studied, not where they must occur. Many sessions open without any Judas Swing at all.

30
00:03:32,816 --> 00:03:41,528
Here is the relationship with Daily Bias. On the left, the daily chart sets a bullish bias, with buy-side liquidity as the draw.

31
00:03:41,948 --> 00:03:49,556
On the right, the session’s first move goes the other way. Price falls below the midnight open and takes sell-side liquidity.

32
00:03:49,976 --> 00:04:00,344
The initial move creates the liquidity, and the better price, before the expected direction. Displacement and a structure shift then provide additional evidence.

33
00:04:00,764 --> 00:04:08,324
In a bearish bias, everything inverts: the first move goes up, takes buy-side liquidity, then fails.

34
00:04:10,144 --> 00:04:17,272
This comparison is mandatory. A liquidity sweep is simply price trading through a relevant high or low.

35
00:04:17,692 --> 00:04:22,732
It can happen in any context, and by itself it does not imply a reversal.

36
00:04:23,152 --> 00:04:25,744
It does not require any session model.

37
00:04:26,164 --> 00:04:32,308
A Judas Swing is an ICT-specific interpretation of an initial deceptive move.

38
00:04:32,728 --> 00:04:37,432
It is discussed within session-based delivery, and it often involves a sweep.

39
00:04:37,852 --> 00:04:42,724
But it also needs a Daily Bias, relevant timing, and later confirmation.

40
00:04:43,144 --> 00:04:50,080
So: a Judas Swing may involve a liquidity sweep, but not every liquidity sweep is a Judas Swing.

41
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:59,436
How does a Judas Swing differ from a classic false breakout? They look similar: a level breaks, then price returns.

42
00:04:59,856 --> 00:05:11,208
The methodology differs. False breakouts, or bull and bear traps, belong to general technical analysis. Judas Swing belongs to ICT’s session-based model.

43
00:05:11,628 --> 00:05:16,092
Liquidity is central to a Judas Swing. Which liquidity was run?

44
00:05:16,512 --> 00:05:21,624
Timing matters: it happens early in a session, measured from a defined open.

45
00:05:22,044 --> 00:05:26,460
A Daily Bias is required, because the move must be counter to it.

46
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:35,448
And what follows matters: displacement and expansion in the bias direction. So, not every false breakout is a Judas Swing.

47
00:05:37,268 --> 00:05:44,804
Now the failures. Failure one: the initial move sweeps liquidity and simply continues in the same direction.

48
00:05:45,224 --> 00:05:51,344
Failure two: the sweep happens, but no displacement follows. Price drifts sideways.

49
00:05:51,764 --> 00:06:00,956
Failure three: price bounces, but never breaks the last lower high. No meaningful structure shift develops, and the decline resumes.

50
00:06:01,376 --> 00:06:09,752
There are two more. Price can remain in consolidation all session, or the assumed Daily Bias itself can become invalid.

51
00:06:10,172 --> 00:06:15,644
This is why the initial sweep should never be treated as an entry signal by itself.

52
00:06:17,464 --> 00:06:22,264
Common mistakes. Calling every liquidity sweep a Judas Swing.

53
00:06:22,684 --> 00:06:24,388
Ignoring session timing.

54
00:06:24,808 --> 00:06:26,800
Ignoring the Daily Bias.

55
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:30,844
Entering before there is evidence of a directional shift.

56
00:06:31,264 --> 00:06:36,304
Assuming every initial move is deceptive. Many first moves are genuine.

57
00:06:36,724 --> 00:06:40,924
Treating the concept as proof of institutional manipulation.

58
00:06:41,344 --> 00:06:47,368
And ignoring alternative explanations, such as news, or a genuine change in sentiment.

59
00:06:49,188 --> 00:06:57,228
To summarise. A Judas Swing is an initial, counter-bias move early in a session, measured from a defined open.

60
00:06:57,648 --> 00:07:04,464
It usually sweeps liquidity, but it also needs bias, timing, displacement and structure.

61
00:07:04,884 --> 00:07:09,732
Not every sweep, and not every false breakout, is a Judas Swing.

62
00:07:10,152 --> 00:07:12,816
No confirmation, no Judas Swing.

63
